    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088",
      "id": "q-38770",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-38770",
        "knowledge_point_id": "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/cd19717bd41b787346a96394ce3bc4e38fe690340bb73bd3d11139d41ff977e2.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n- $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-1$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
        "answer_fields": [
