    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088",
      "id": "q-80348",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-80348",
        "knowledge_point_id": "4fc09a0f-34d3-5ca9-9964-6cb00498bfa7",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Factored Polynomial Shown in a Graph",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932088/assets/8b086759a25af7e464c0c8be78d36cd4d8458c5c25e7c84cb6ecb28b2d59e9df.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?",
        "worked_solution": "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-1$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-1,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=0,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=0,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=2,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y={x}^{3}(x+1){(x-2)}^{3}.$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "y={x}^{2}(x+1){(x-2)}^{2}",
                "option": "0",
                "source_option": "a"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "y={x}^{3}(x+1)(x-2)",
                "option": "1",
                "source_option": "b"
              },
