        "knowledge_point_id": "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation",
        "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-{135}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
        "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{135}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{135}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={225}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${225}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{135}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932272/assets/1196aea617827f2883e059747f037997741570eac382b309391a88978a861246.png)\n\nWe see that ${225}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant III. Therefore, $-{135}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
        "answer_fields": [
