    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932272",
      "id": "q-113743",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-113743",
        "knowledge_point_id": "078c9361-898f-5b9f-889e-c92442f882d9",
        "knowledge_point": "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function",
        "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y.$",
        "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
